  safe supply programs so this is when we  prescribe opioids so that they're not  tainted  and these folks might have a higher  prevalence of pepsi so knowing  immediately if they are actively  infected  could decrease the time from diagnosis  to  treatment  another thought would be to use core  antigen testing for  people that we already know are antibody  positive so looking back at our  databases if we have them or we already  know their status and maybe they've even  been treated and so we could look at  this core antigen test from a test of  cure perspective or using for svr  or looking for reinfection so in that  population that are known antibody  positive because they've been diagnosed  they've maybe been treated and you have  a high degree of suspicion that they  failed therapy or they're potentially  reinfected which we're seeing so much  coming out of the pandemic  using core antigen as that re-screen for  active infection  and again just to speak to the fact that  the costs might be so high for looking  for reinfection using nucleic acid based  testing or pcr based testing that this  is a time when we really want to  consider using core antigen testing  i just like to end with a couple of  conclusion slides so in terms of our  model we really look at this that we  need to test and treat people where they  are at in life and location so our work  really incorporates every single aspect  of social services  um looking at things like training  primary care providers making them aware  of different modalities for testing  diagnosis and treatment and in primary  care thinking about things like you know  testing every new patient  using it in the context of i'm already  ordering blood work so i'll order hep c  screening thinking about people that do  have risk factors  and thinking about people that have  other types of liver disease like  alcohol or fatty liver  in terms of a mental health or  corrections scenario so certainly using  core antigen testing in a region that  have a high prevalence or a location  that would have a high prevalence like  corrections  could certainly make sense  some inpatient mental health studies  have shown that prevalence can be as  high as 15 percent and that it would  hold true in community mental health  settings and of course much higher in  addiction settings  in terms of outreach so this is what i  mean by meeting people where they're at  and life and location hep c might not be  on their radar whatsoever  but if we can work with them in terms of  where they're at in shelter in drop-ins  and pharmacies we've sent seen the  incredible impact in canada of engaging  our pharmacy colleagues to do screening  and testing for covin and we should be  able to do the same for hepatitis c  thinking about doing home visits gosh  you know driving to someone's house and  having them tested in the comfort of  their own home looking at newcomer and  immigrant social services sites where we  can work with folks and then working  with people who have experienced  violence who are very unlikely to seek  care and might have been stigmatized in  the past  and then finally thinking about your  tertiary care settings not to leave  tertiary care settings out but to think  about them in a different way so in the  past we thought about tertiary care  settings as the place where we would  diagnose and treat hepatitis c and now  we can be looking at tertiary care  settings as places where we can actually  screen universally for hep c so thinking  about emergency rooms and then other  populations which will have overlapping  health complications um from hepatitis c  like our diabetic population who most  often has fatty liver disease that  obviously treating them for hepatitis c  would be advantageous looking at those  in renal failure in cardiology clinics  and then you know even thinking about  out of the box thinking like obstetrical  practices geriatric practices and  pediatric practices  of course in the context of testing it's  not a one-size-fits-all approach we  really need to evaluate these individual  scenarios these individual settings for  what's the best algorithm for each of  these populations because that's really  what this is all about this is about  meeting the individuals and working with  them in the way that they want to  receive care  i'll just end off with a couple of sort  of take-home conclusions around that  screening and diagnosis  aspect of the talk so one thought would  be around optimizing testing and how can  we do that well we really should think  hard about incorporating core androgen  into our testing algorithms so moving  from  antibody positivity to  reflex testing this helps us keep this  in this serological wheelhouse  when hep c rna testing is not readily  available when samples need to be  shipped or when it's too expensive  thinking about core antigen testing as a  first screening tool i really do hope we  get there i hope we can wrap our heads  around using core antigen or other  measures of active infection for an  initial test because in a population  that has a very high prevalence of  antibody positivity it might not be that  helpful to be screening these folks by  antibody testing and then in terms of  repeat testing so we could be using  coriantogen  to establish chronicity as well as  looking at  test of cure and then testing for  reinfection  i did present some work from our group  today and so i just wanted to  acknowledge many of the authors that  were involved in the work that i  presented  and again just to thank abbott and lab  roots for hosting this webinar today  it's been an absolute privilege to be  here with you and i'd be happy to take  any questions  thank you dr biandi for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now just click on the ask a  question box located on the far left of  your screen  we'll answer as 