additionally covid19 has put tremendous  pressure on our health care system  and on the diagnostic system that we had  in place so there might be some  advantages that we can see from that so  we've created really good lab-based  testing pathways to optimize screening  for covid19 and so one thought in terms  of recovery would be to leverage some of  these pathways including new equipment  and  use those same testing pathways and  those same pieces of equipment for  increasing our ability to screen and  test for hep c i think in an outrage  outreach setting if you take nothing  else home from my talk today it's that  reflex testing is essential  thank you  next question  if  parents are infected  how do you prevent  their baby or their child  from having hepatitis  yeah so unlike hepatitis b  household transmission  is not as common  and so outside of those pieces that i  mentioned about toothbrushes  sharing nail clippers sharing tweezers  sharing things that could have  small amounts of blood  household transmission  is not common  however  when we think about parents and we think  about pregnancy  vertical transmission  is actually five percent and in the  context of an hiv hepatitis c  co-infection can approach  11 to 12 percent  we just published a study to show that  there's a potential that viral load  could actually  increase the potential for vertical  transmission so in the context of  parents i think what we're really  exploring now is around women who have  hepatitis c in pregnancy is  should we be  intervening in pregnancy so not just  screening to know but should we actually  consider treatment in pregnancy and if  we're not comfortable considering  treatment in pregnancy we definitely  need to treat in the postpartum period  as we understand it right now  most women are not treated in the  postpartum period before they become  pregnant again so we could treat  postpartum and then eliminate any risk  of ongoing  transmission in terms of that vertical  